WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.320
(audience applauding)

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.210
- [Narrator] At the Annual
Federal Reserve Symposium

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:06.570
in 2022, just a few months
after the Federal Reserve

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:09.330
began to raise interest
rates to fight inflation,

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.010
Chairman Jay Powell was clear

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.710
about what those actions
might do to the economy.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:14.970
- While higher interest rates,

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:18.030
slower growth and softer
labor market conditions

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.010
will bring down inflation,

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:23.190
they will also bring some pain
to households and businesses.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.130
These are the unfortunate
costs of reducing inflation.

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.870
- He didn't mince any words.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.700
He was quite blunt in the fact

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.400
that the Fed was willing
to take a recession

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.460
if that's what it took
to derail inflation,

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:36.540
and there was a sense

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.480
that that's what they
believed was necessary.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.520
- [Narrator] Of course,
the Fed is trying for-

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.710
- A soft or softish landing.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.870
It's a soft landing with
inflation coming down

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.640
and unemployment holding steady.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:48.540
- [Narrator] But by fall,

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.730
more than 60% of economists surveyed

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:53.160
by "The Wall Street Journal"
including Diane Swonk

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.590
predicted a recession in the coming year.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:58.710
But now, almost a year
later, it still hasn't come.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.870
- I am more than happy
to eat crow on this one.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.300
When you're wrong and the
economy is more resilient,

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:04.680
that's a wonderful thing.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.830
- [Narrator] So where
did the recession go?

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.620
A recession starts in
one of these three areas.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.240
Either people spend less money,

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.960
leading businesses to make
less money and lay people off,

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.030
or they start when people get laid off,

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.470
giving them less spending power

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.360
and businesses fewer profits.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.400
Or an industry may start to do poorly

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.440
and the cycle flows from there.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:28.320
But all of these areas
were strong back in 2022

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.260
when the rate hike started,
and they still are.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.880
There's a few reasons why.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.260
Let's start with spending.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.020
Simply put, there's a
lot of money out there.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.690
People had a lot in savings
coming out of the pandemic,

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:40.800
thanks to the stimulus,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.800
and generally just not
spending money in 2020,

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.990
which meant that when they
started spending again,

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.540
they had that cash despite inflation.

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.690
- We also had the lingering
effects of the stimulus,

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.970
which ramped up at the
state and local level

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.680
on infrastructure spending.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:56.670
- [Narrator] Money is also there

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.220
because people still have jobs.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.290
The unemployment rate continues to hold

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.360
at pre-pandemic levels.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.680
That reflects two things,

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.890
a lower percentage of Americans
working or looking for work

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.530
thanks to a wave of
baby boomer retirements,

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.800
and there are still jobs, a lot of jobs.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.050
The number of vacancies
is still far higher

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:19.890
than it was pre-pandemic,
which also keeps wages growing.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.890
July, 2023 was the first time in two years

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:26.100
where wages grew faster than inflation.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.380
So people were spending money and working

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.990
and businesses were in a strong position.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.850
Not only did corporate profits rise,

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.470
they had less debt to be affected

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.390
by the Fed's rising interest rates.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:39.720
- Large corporations either
locked into low rates

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.640
or carried much less debt.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:42.990
And that's really important

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:46.440
because that also acted
as yet another buffer

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:50.430
against higher rates and the
implications for employment.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.650
- [Narrator] A major way
this cycle is often set off,

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.780
especially when the Fed
raises interest rates,

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.370
is the housing market.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.650
But this one is different.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.660
Normally, when the Fed raises rates,

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.040
that increases mortgage rates,

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.800
which slows the number of new houses sold,

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.540
slowing construction and furnishing

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:07.920
and all the spending and jobs

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.140
that come from a robust housing market.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.000
Blue goes up, red goes down,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.220
and usually a recession follows.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.590
But that's not really happening now.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.020
The number of new houses
being sold has slowed,

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.700
but it was already slowing

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.453
thanks to a really limited
supply and what happened here.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:30.030
More than 90% of all homeowners
have a rate below 6%.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.450
More than 60% have a rate below 4%.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.820
- It caused what we
called a mortgage winter

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.700
where people were
literally locked in place

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.220
because they couldn't
afford to now buy a house

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.650
at a higher interest rate
and much higher prices.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:45.840
However, they were also hedged

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:50.370
against both inflation and
those interest rate hikes.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:51.510
- [Narrator] Now, here's
where we must put in

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:52.560
the disclaimer.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.440
Just because there hasn't
been a recession in 2022

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.100
or the first half of 2023
doesn't mean there won't be one.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.200
- There are headwinds that are building

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.420
that I think are quite substantial.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:04.980
- [Narrator] Sure,
people had money to spend

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.050
coming out of the pandemic, but today,

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:10.983
people have way less saved,
and they're more in debt.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.760
Economists are still unsure
how major Hollywood strikes

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.850
will affect unemployment numbers
or the economy as a whole.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.230
And don't forget inflation,

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:22.320
which is still not down to
the Federal Reserve's target.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.080
Now, most economists
still predict a recession,

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.520
but fewer of them.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:27.900
- I am much more optimistic

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.480
than I was that we can weather the storm.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.000
I think it's very important
that at the press conference,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.710
that Chairman Powell was careful

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.870
to not use the word optimism.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:39.837
- I wouldn't use the term
optimism about this yet.

00:04:39.837 --> 00:04:43.410
- He said there is a
path to a soft landing

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:46.680
and I think there's a wider
path to a soft landing

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:47.790
than we once thought.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.550
- It's a long way from
a short and, you know,

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:53.247
we have a lot left to
go to see that happen.

00:04:53.247 --> 00:04:56.164
(intriguing music)

